  "correction": {
    "what_was_wrong": "The first cut judged condition 3 on general30 alone and reported v-tts epoch 2 and epoch 5 as meeting the absolute bar. Judged across all three prompt sets - held-out, seen and general30 - NO arm meets it, including those two.",
    "how_it_was_missed": "general30 hides the failure mode. v-real/epoch4 is silent on 0 of 30 general30 prompts and on 8 of 10 held-out and 8 of 10 seen. The prompt sets differ: general30 has a speaking user inside the generated window, the voice sets have a silent partner, and the degraded checkpoints only fall silent against the silent partner.",
    "second_defect": "tools/dialogue_collapse.py returns monologue_loop=False and exact_repeat_collapse=False for a silent generation - verified on v-real/epoch5/held-out/2.npy. So the absolute bar gets EASIER as an arm goes silent. Reporting the two collapse counts without the silence count beside them, as the first cut did in its headline, makes a mute checkpoint look clean.",
    "found_by": "the adversarial critique of the local scoring plan, not by the original pass"
  },
