  "status": "NOT met in the absolute form. v-tts/epoch2 is no worse than the control on every count; that is the strongest defensible claim.",
  "finding": {
    "headline": "V-real degrades into silence as training proceeds; V-tts does not.",
    "silent_share": {
      "v-real": [
        "0%",
        "0%",
        "14%",
        "32%",
        "48%"
      ],
      "v-tts": [
        "0%",
        "0%",
        "2%",
        "14%",
        "12%"
      ],
      "control": "0%"
    },
    "reading": "Speaker A in V-real is 8.67 minutes across 72 sentences. Five epochs over that is enough to push the model toward saying nothing at all - by epoch 5 it is silent on 24 of 50 prompts. The same schedule on V-tts, whose channel A is synthetic but otherwise identical in scripts, split, speaker B and timeline, stays at 12%.",
    "caveat": "This does not isolate 'real versus synthetic audio'. V-tts's channel A is also more uniform - one TTS voice with consistent loudness and no recording-session variation - and that uniformity may be what the model tolerates, not the synthesis itself."
  },
  "limits": [
    "Silence is scored from the text stream. A generation that emits no text is flagged, which is the intended behaviour: saying nothing is not health.",
    "Partner-channel activity is deliberately excluded, being teacher-forced and therefore vacuous.",
    "A silent generation trips neither collapse flag, so the absolute bar rewards going mute. The silence count must always be reported beside the collapse counts.",
    "Judging on general30 alone hides silence that appears only against a silent partner. All three prompt sets are required."
  ]
}
